{"ON the morning following the events just narrated, Mrs. Arlington was seated at breakfast in a sweet little parlour of the splendid mansion which the Earl of Warrington had taken and fitted up for her in Dover Street, Piccadilly. It was about \003eleven o’clock\004; and the Enchantress was attired in a delicious deshabillé. With her little feet upon an ottoman near the fender, and her fine form reclining in a luxurious large arm-chair, she divided her attention between her chocolate and the columns of the Morning Herald.","The Mysteries of London","G.W.M. Reynolds",true},
{"Quiet as I am, I become at \003Eleven o’Clock\004 in the Morning on every day of the week save Sunday a raving, ranting maniac — a dangerous lunatic, panting with insane desires to do, not only myself but other people, a mischief, and possessed less by hallucination than by rabies.","Twice Around the Clock","George Augustus Sala",true},
{"Though perhaps – but here the bracket clock whirred and then hectically struck \003eleven\004, its weights spooling downwards at the sudden expense of energy. She had to sit for a moment, when the echo had vanished, to repossess her thoughts.","The Stranger’s Child","Alan Hollinghurst",true},
{"We got to Waterloo at \003eleven\004, and asked where the eleven-five started from. Of course nobody knew; nobody at Waterloo ever does know where a train is going to start from, or where a train when it does start is going to, or anything about it.","Three Men in a Boat","Jerome K. Jerome",true},
{"We passed a few sad hours until \003eleven o’clock\004, when the trial was to commence. My father and the rest of the family being obliged to attend as witnesses, I accompanied them to the court. During the whole of this wretched mockery of justice I suffered living torture.","Frankenstein","Mary Shelley",true},
